---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="Foundation">
  <h1>Foundation</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    What a design system actually is, the layers it's built from, and the principles that keep it
    coherent as it grows.
  </p>

  <h2>Definition</h2>
  <p>
    A design system is a shared language for building products: a set of reusable parts plus the
    standards for using them. It is not a component library alone — the library is one artifact. A
    reasonably complete system contains:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Design tokens</strong> — named decisions (color, type, space, motion) stored as data.</li>
    <li><strong>Components</strong> — coded, accessible building blocks with documented APIs.</li>
    <li><strong>Patterns</strong> — compositions of components that solve recurring product problems.</li>
    <li><strong>Guidelines</strong> — usage, content, and accessibility guidance around the parts.</li>
    <li><strong>Governance</strong> — the people, processes, and versioning that keep it alive.</li>
    <li><strong>Tooling</strong> — Figma libraries, lint rules, CI checks, documentation site.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The layer model</h2>
  <p>
    Most mature systems converge on a layered architecture. Each layer only depends on the one
    below it, which is what makes theming and change management tractable:
  </p>
  <pre><code>foundations   raw values: hex codes, px, font files
   ↓
tokens        named decisions: color.text.primary, space.200
   ↓
primitives    unstyled behavior: focus trap, popover positioning
   ↓
components    Button, TextField, Dialog — token-styled, ARIA-correct
   ↓
patterns      form layout, filtering, onboarding, error recovery
   ↓
product       features composed from the layers above</code></pre>
  <p>
    The layering echoes Brad Frost's atomic design (atoms → molecules → organisms), but teams name
    the layers differently. What matters is the dependency direction: product code shouldn't reach
    past components into raw values, and components shouldn't hard-code what tokens can express.
  </p>

  <h2>Principles worth writing down</h2>
  <p>
    Principles only earn their keep when they settle arguments. Common ones that do real work:
  </p>
  <dl>
    <dt>Accessible by default</dt>
    <dd>
      Conformance (WCAG 2.2 AA is the common target) is built into components, not left to product
      teams to bolt on.
    </dd>
    <dt>Composition over configuration</dt>
    <dd>
      Prefer small parts that combine over god-components with 40 props. See
      <a href="/components/">Components</a> for the trade-off.
    </dd>
    <dt>Platform-native first</dt>
    <dd>
      Use the HTML element or platform behavior before re-implementing it. A styled
      <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> beats a <code>&lt;div role="button"&gt;</code> every time.
    </dd>
    <dt>Documented or it doesn't exist</dt>
    <dd>A component without usage docs, props table, and a11y notes isn't shipped, it's leaked.</dd>
    <dt>Changes are cheap, breaks are expensive</dt>
    <dd>Additive evolution over rewrites; deprecate loudly, remove slowly. See <a href="/governance/">Governance</a>.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>What the UX engineer owns</h2>
  <p>
    At senior/principal level, the role sits at the seam between design and engineering. Typical
    ownership:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The fidelity of the translation: design intent → tokens → component code.</li>
    <li>Component API quality and consistency across the library.</li>
    <li>Accessibility conformance of the shipped parts, with evidence (tests, audits).</li>
    <li>The decision record: why a pattern exists, what was rejected, what it costs.</li>
    <li>Enablement: docs, examples, office hours, code review for adopting teams.</li>
  </ul>

